# Which uncertainties can more money actually fix?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a clear, warm university lecturer: measured, engaged, never theatrical","b":"gemini:Charon | a clear, warm university lecturer: measured, engaged, never theatrical"}
next: {"title":"Showing what you don't know: from tornado diagrams to acceptability curves","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/health-technology-assessment-deciding/showing-what-you-don-t-know-from-tornado.cast"}

## Four Unknowns, One Decision
voice: male
level: advanced

When a committee says the evidence is too uncertain, more research sounds like the obvious cure — this series asks which unknowns money can actually buy down.
    text title "Nusinersen for SMA — the appraisal sheet" x 500 y 700 font_size 30
    text icer_line "ICER = ΔC / ΔE" x 500 y 560 font_size 30

For nusinersen in spinal muscular atrophy, the incremental cost per QALY came out in the millions of kroner.
    text icer_num "millions of kroner per QALY gained" #b5482e x 500 y 495 font_size 26

    pause 0.3

Against a severity-weighted threshold near eight hundred thousand kroner per QALY, that is nowhere close.
    text lambda_line "λ, severity-weighted ≈ 825 000 kr per QALY" #2f6b8f x 500 y 420 font_size 26

Beslutningsforum said no in 2017, then yes in 2018 — but only for patients below a certain age.
    text verdict_line "Beslutningsforum: no (2017) → yes (2018), with an age limit" x 500 y 330 font_size 24

Behind that single ratio sit four unknowns, with one word stretched across all of them.
    text one_number "One number. Four different unknowns behind it." #b5482e x 500 y 230 font_size 26
    delivery grave

    pause 0.5

Open the sheet, and they come apart.
    clear keep ["title"]

First, a survival hazard — how long treated infants live, estimated from roughly a hundred and twenty babies followed about a year.
    shape box1 #8f887c shape rect x 290 y 545 width 380 height 130
    text b1_h "1. A survival hazard" x 290 y 580 font_size 23
    text b1_d "Estimated from ~120 infants" x 290 y 545 font_size 19
    text b1_d2 "followed for roughly a year" x 290 y 515 font_size 19

    pause 0.3

Second, the shape of the model itself: three motor states or five, and whether a milestone once gained can be lost again.
    shape box2 #8f887c shape rect x 710 y 545 width 380 height 130
    text b2_h "2. The model's shape" x 710 y 580 font_size 23
    text b2_d "Three motor states, or five?" x 710 y 545 font_size 19
    text b2_d2 "Can a gained milestone be lost?" x 710 y 515 font_size 19

    pause 0.3

Third, who the patient is — a three-month-old with type one is simply not the same person as a fourteen-year-old with type two.
    shape box3 #8f887c shape rect x 290 y 365 width 380 height 130
    text b3_h "3. Who the patient is" x 290 y 400 font_size 23
    text b3_d "A 3-month-old with type 1, or" x 290 y 365 font_size 19
    text b3_d2 "a 14-year-old with type 2?" x 290 y 335 font_size 19

    pause 0.3

Fourth, the discount rate: four percent a year by Norwegian guideline, compounded across sixty years of a life.
    shape box4 #8f887c shape rect x 710 y 365 width 380 height 130
    text b4_h "4. The discount rate" x 710 y 400 font_size 23
    text b4_d "r = 4% a year, by guideline" x 710 y 365 font_size 19
    text b4_d2 "compounded over 60 years" x 710 y 335 font_size 19

    pause 0.5

And the age limit that unlocked the yes was the committee's answer to the third row — never to the first.
    text note1 "The age limit answered row 3," #8f887c x 290 y 245 font_size 18
    text note2 "not row 1." #8f887c x 290 y 220 font_size 18

So the question the series turns on: which of these four would a new trial remove?
    text question "Which of these would a new trial remove?" #b5482e x 620 y 205 font_size 27
    mark q_mark target question kind box

Only one of them has a sample size attached.
    highlight box1 b1_h b1_d b1_d2 effect glow

The other three would read exactly the same the morning after that trial reports.
    focus box2 b2_h b2_d b2_d2 box3 b3_h b3_d b3_d2 box4 b4_h b4_d b4_d2

One word — uncertainty — doing four different jobs, and only one of them is for sale.
    delivery grave

## Sorting the Unknowns: A Four-Box Taxonomy
voice: male
level: advanced
use: two_by_two_table
with:
    title: Four unknowns, sorted by what would fix them
    row_label: What is unknown
    col_label: Can more data fix it?
    row_values:
      - The inputs
      - The frame
    col_values:
      - 'Yes'
      - 'No'
    cells:
      - [Parameter, Heterogeneity]
      - [Structural, Methodological]
    cell_notes:
      - [the value of an input, patients genuinely differ]
      - [the shape of the model, the rules of the analysis]
    highlight:
      - [0, 0]
      - [1, 0]
    box:
      x: 60
      'y': 175
      w: 880
      h: 490

Four unknowns pull at the same nusinersen decision, and the practical question is which of them another study could actually buy away — because committees are routinely asked to wait for evidence that would never have helped.
    draw title grid

Sort them first by what is unknown: the numbers we feed the model, or the frame we built around those numbers.
    draw row_title row_header_0 row_header_1

And second by whether evidence — another trial, longer follow-up — could ever shrink it.
    draw col_title col_header_0 col_header_1

    pause 0.3

Top left: our model needs a yearly probability of losing ventilator-free survival, and the data put it near 0.12, consistent with anything from 0.08 to 0.17.
    draw cell_0_0

A true value exists and our estimate is merely noisy — quadruple the sample and the interval halves.
    focus cell_0_0

    pause 0.4

Top right is still numbers, but a different animal: a six-month-old with type 1 disease and a thirty-year-old with type 3 do not share one transition probability.
    draw cell_0_1

A thousand more patients would not shrink that spread by a hair — which is why Beslutningsforum, the decision forum, answered nusinersen with an age limit rather than a bigger study.
    point at.ref cell_0_1 gesture circle

    pause 0.4

Bottom left leaves the inputs alone and asks about the frame: does the benefit hold for life or fade after ten years, and does the model even contain a state where it fades?
    draw cell_1_0

Two models with identical parameters can hand you ICERs that differ by a factor — settleable in principle, but only by twenty years of follow-up nobody has.
    focus cell_1_0

Which is why this box is the one most often left unquantified — named in the discussion section, given a number far less often than the parameters above it.
    highlight cell_1_0 effect glow

    pause 0.3

Bottom right: the four percent discount rate, the perspective, whose costs count, lambda itself — rules chosen by guideline, never measured by anyone.
    draw cell_1_1

And in the boring contrast case, statins, this box dominates: costs land now and health lands decades later, so moving the discount rate shifts the ICER further than any confidence interval on the effect ever could.
    focus cell_1_1

    pause 0.4

So a new trial writes only in this left column — quickly at the top, slowly and only with time at the bottom.
    draw hl_0_0 hl_1_0 parallel true

Money buys evidence, and evidence reaches exactly this far — the right-hand column survives every trial ever funded.
    mark mark_col target col_header_0 kind box
    delivery grave

And heterogeneity was never waiting for evidence — it is waiting for us to stop averaging over it, which is where we go next.
    highlight cell_0_1 effect glow

## Heterogeneity is not uncertainty
voice: male
level: advanced
use: cost_effectiveness_plane
with:
    title: 'Nusinersen: one average, two realities'
    x_label: ΔE (QALYs gained)
    y_label: ΔC (kr)
    wtp_threshold: 1000000
    points:
      - {label: 'Child, treated early', effect: 8, cost: 4800000}
      - {label: 'Adult, established disease', effect: 2, cost: 4800000}
      - {label: The average patient, effect: 5, cost: 4800000, emphasize: true}

Of the four boxes, take the one that was never uncertainty at all — and ask: which patient does an average ICER actually describe?
    draw x_axis y_axis

Here is the threshold line: at Norway's severity-weighted ceiling, roughly a million kroner per QALY, anything below it is worth buying.
    draw wtp_line wtp_label

    pause 0.3

Nusinersen in a child treated early gains about eight QALYs for 4.8 million kroner — an ICER near 600,000 per QALY, comfortably under the line.
    draw pt_0 pt_label_0

    pause 0.3

Same drug, same price, an adult with established motor neurone loss: about two QALYs — 2.4 million kroner per QALY, well above it.
    draw pt_1 pt_label_1

These are not two guesses at one number — both are true, and we know in advance which patient is which.
    highlight pt_0 pt_1 effect glow

    pause 0.4

Now pool them, half and half: five QALYs on average, still 4.8 million kroner, so 960,000 per QALY.
    draw pt_2 pt_label_2

That slips in just under the threshold, so the average says yes — to everybody.
    point at.ref pt_2 gesture circle delivery grave

But nobody gains five QALYs. The average patient is the one patient the trial never enrolled.
    mark cross_avg target pt_label_2 kind cross

Strip the average away, and the two real groups are still standing exactly where they were.
    clear keep ["x_axis","y_axis","wtp_line","wtp_label","pt_0","pt_label_0","pt_1","pt_label_1"]

Run a trial ten times larger and each dot tightens into a smaller cloud — that is parameter uncertainty shrinking, and money buys it.
    highlight pt_0 pt_1 effect pulse

The distance between the dots does not shrink at all: it is a fact about the patients, not about our sample size.
    focus pt_0 pt_1

In 2018 Beslutningsforum did precisely this — yes to nusinersen, but only for patients under eighteen.
    mark box_kids target pt_label_0 kind box

And, painfully, no to adults with the same diagnosis; that age limit is a refusal to average.
    highlight pt_1 effect glow

So the answer to our question: the average ICER describes nobody — and no trial, however large, will ever make it describe someone.
    draw title

## What survives every trial
voice: male
level: advanced

    box n_param "Parameter uncertainty" #2f6b8f x 240 y 600 width 300 hidden true
    box n_struct "Structural uncertainty" x 240 y 490 width 300 hidden true
    box n_hetero "Heterogeneity" #8a5fa8 x 240 y 250 width 300 hidden true
    box n_method "Methodological uncertainty" #b5482e x 240 y 380 width 300 hidden true
    box f_shrink "Evidence can shrink it" #2f6b8f x 750 y 545 width 300 hidden true
    box f_split "Only stratification handles it" #8a5fa8 x 750 y 250 width 300 hidden true
    box f_survive "Survives every trial" #b5482e x 750 y 380 width 300 hidden true
    edge e_param n_param -> f_shrink #2f6b8f hidden true
    edge e_struct_shrink n_struct -> f_shrink curved #2f6b8f hidden true
    edge e_struct_survive n_struct -> f_survive curved #b5482e hidden true
    edge e_hetero n_hetero -> f_split #8a5fa8 hidden true
    edge e_method n_method -> f_survive #b5482e hidden true
    label lbl_struct_shrink "Does the effect persist?" above #2f6b8f attach_to e_struct_shrink hidden true
    label lbl_struct_survive "80-year extrapolation" below #b5482e attach_to e_struct_survive hidden true

Averaging hides heterogeneity; put all four unknowns together — and one question: which of them can more money actually fix?
    draw n_param n_struct n_hetero n_method parallel true

    pause 0.4

The first fate: unknowns that genuinely shrink when someone goes out and collects more data.
    draw f_shrink

Parameter uncertainty goes straight here — in our nusinersen model the utility of a treated child was 0.40, with a confidence interval from 0.25 to 0.55.
    draw e_param

Quadruple the sample and that interval halves; this is the one box a cheque can honestly buy down.
    highlight e_param effect glow

    pause 0.3

Part of structural uncertainty is testable too — whether the treatment effect persists after year five is something long follow-up can settle.
    draw e_struct_shrink lbl_struct_shrink

But the rest of the structure walks into a second fate: the things no trial ever reaches.
    draw f_survive

Our Markov model runs a child treated at six months out over eighty years, from five years of evidence — nobody will ever run the study that checks that tail.
    draw e_struct_survive lbl_struct_survive

    pause 0.3

Methodological uncertainty settles there permanently: the four percent discount rate, the perspective, the threshold, whether health should be counted in QALYs at all.
    draw e_method

Heterogeneity gets a fate of its own — it is real variation, not ignorance, so it cannot shrink; you can only stop averaging and split the decision.
    draw f_split e_hetero

Beslutningsforum, the national decision forum, did exactly that: nusinersen was rejected, then accepted with an age limit at eighteen — one technology, two answers, by subgroup.
    highlight f_split effect glow

    pause 0.4

So box the bottom of that column: this is the residue, everything still standing after the perfect trial.
    mark mark_residue target f_survive kind box

Imagine that perfect trial has been run — ten thousand infants, thirty years of follow-up, every parameter nailed down.
    clear keep ["n_method","f_survive","mark_residue"]

You would know the QALY gain to three decimals, and still not know these three.
    text res_lambda "Threshold λ" #b5482e x 750 y 560 font_size 24
    text res_r "Discount rate r = 4%" #b5482e x 750 y 510 font_size 24
    text res_persp "Perspective: whose costs count" #b5482e x 750 y 460 font_size 24
    parallel true

At a threshold of 275,000 kroner per QALY nusinersen is far out of reach; at 1.4 million it is arguable — and no experiment decides that, because lambda describes the health we displace, not the drug.
    highlight res_lambda effect glow delivery grave

Across the four parts: parameters shrink with evidence, structure only in part, heterogeneity splits rather than shrinks, and the methodological core survives every trial ever run.
    highlight f_survive effect pulse

More money buys precision about the world; it never buys agreement about what the world is worth — and next time we look at how to show that residue honestly.

## Where next
